ed shooting sense it's now  171 hours of programming Wow yeah now  did you would along sort of career or  personal life of building stuff working  in special  facts but had you been interested up  until that moment in actually trying to  debunk myths and sort of like unbilled  things and no the myth part is a  scarecrow everyone we always say  nobody's ever emailed us thanking us for  the groundbreaking work and urban legend  research we're doing I definitely feel  at this point we have a real insight  into what makes urban legends and great  stories propagate but for the most part  it's a terrific framework on which to  hang a narrative that's always about  curiosity it's always about curiosity  and it turns out that the best way to  satisfy your curiosity happens to be the  scientific method it happens to be see  what you can learn from doing this build  on that they'll build the next thing and  the scientific method happens  fortuitously for us to grid perfectly on  top of a narrative arc and I mean that's  the arc of every episode is we're trying  to figure out how something works by  continually testing it and building our  knowledge on what we've learned before  and we're still thrilled by doing that  in the process of doing that has it sort  of changed your ideas about the  propagation of myths or about society in  the way people has it changed your  politics in any way it hasn't changed my  politics except to make me more  politically active because I realized  that I'm a voice that people that people  pay attention to I am I'm very cognizant  now of the role of the very unexpected  role we play in educating children I  mean we don't pander to kids and we  never set out to make a show that was  educational really I mean that we  understand that that's what it is but if  we attempted to do that we would have  failed there's a veracity to the fact  that we are actually curious about what  we're doing that keeps people watching  and we're now getting emails from people  saying that they're graduating from  college having grown up watching our  show I've been inspired to go into the  sciences because they watch Mythbusters  which is I mean it's humbling and  bizarre and amazing and thrilling what's  the sort of future of this you're just  going to keep doing it until you get  bored or they kick you off the show is  still doing fantastic  we are still leading our demo in our  time slot on discovery on advertising  supported cable after seven years our  viewership stays beautifully beautifully  stable I like to point out that our  ratings died during the presidential  debates but didn't budge during game one  or game seven in the last World Series  oh wow it's very specific viewing  audience I think and the show is still  going strong we want to we'll keep doing  it until they lock the doors and it  doesn't look like there's any end in  sight what are sort of maybe like three  categories if there are three or two or  whatever of types of myths that sort of  obtained in people's consciousness that  you have encountered well we definitely  started out with your kind of laundry  list of urban legends things that you  would have found on the Darwin Awards or  under urban legend sites like snopes com  or the straight dope but we have  branched way out from that realizing  that movie physics movie the way movies  perceive the world and lead you to  believe that every car that goes off a  cliff will blow up into a fireball  that's very fertile territory there's  idioms idiomatic phrases like slipping  on a banana peel finding a needle in the  haystack we're just actually looking at  doing another one where I want to test a  wild goose chase I want to see Jamie  stealing candy from a baby and then  there's a and then there's then there's  the prevailing wisdom about how things  work like the five-second rule which  actually you know is one of those things  when you start to go look at it there  might be something really interesting  there is it as that you drop the  pacifier on the ground if it's only been  there for five seconds we talk about a  three second rule yeah household exactly  it's going down to two in one second we  just did double dipping for instance  that just aired I think this this last  week and we showed that there was no ill  effects from double dipping the amount  of bacteria that you impart back to the  salsa by biting a chip and putting it  back in negligible having worked in  effects and sort of being a participant  in sort of 21st century technologies and  whatnot do you get the sense that people  because they've done their own photo  show  popping and and are on the internet are  they are they given more kind of  skeptical tools now or is it more that  the technology has made it easier to  fabricate things oh that's a good  question I think I mean obviously it's  both I think people are we're always  asked are you guys running out of  material and I mean having just sat down  and gone over 160 stories that will  carry us two seasons ahead at least  we're not running out of stories and we  say we're not going to run out of  stories until people stop believing  stupid [ __ ] and that's never going to  happen people are still just as gullible  and a good store is a good story and you  want to pass it on even if you're not  quite sure it's true I like to imagine  that the web is providing people with  well no actually of course the web is  providing people with more tools to be  skeptical to duck to cross check their  facts not everyone is doing it but  because the web provides so much  information on a first-order retrieve  ability to everybody people can go and  check their facts and they can go I mean  honestly I know that a lot of my  fact-checking has to do with just  reading the style of the writing on  reading on the website I can smell  [ __ ] just by how badly written  something is sometimes it's not  necessarily about you know that the  facts are wrong how many 